# The v3 prelude

Loaded before every program v3 EXECUTES, so the names here need no import. Not loaded when printing
an `Ast` — `Loader.preludeRoot` says why that split is principled rather than convenient.

Keep this module cheap: it is parsed and lowered on every invocation, including twice per case on a
368-file corpus sweep.

## Dataset

`v3/BACKLOG.md`'s DATASET decision, option 3, chosen by the owner: written IN ScalaScript over
lists, so BOTH v3 lanes get one implementation and v3 grows no host surface. Options 1 (implement it
twice, in `Exec.scala` and the shared `v2/src` kernel) and 2 (give `run-ir` a plugin path) were
declined.

**`of` is a vararg, and it took a compiler change to be allowed to write it that way.** It used to
take SIXTEEN defaulted parameters and a `DsAbsent()` sentinel, with sixteen `if x != DsAbsent()`
lines to rebuild the list — because Tier 0 had no varargs, so `def of(items: Any*)` parsed as ONE
parameter and `Dataset.of(1, 2, 3)` was refused with "passes 3 argument(s), it takes 1". There is
still no arity overloading (two `def`s of one name leave the first winning), which is why sixteen
parameters was the shape the workaround had to take.

**Sixteen was a CAP, not a comfortable margin, and it was reachable.** `Dataset.of` with seventeen
elements was refused by a number that came from an implementation detail of the workaround. SSC3-14b
(2026-08-14) added varargs and this is the first thing they buy: `T*` has no cap, and the sentinel,
its `DsAbsent` case class and the sixteen `if`s all go with it — the sentinel existed only to mark a
parameter nobody filled.

**`Dataset` carries its ELEMENT TYPE, and at Tier 0 that is documentation rather than enforcement.**
`def of[T](items: T*): Dataset[T]` is the owner's spelling, and it only reads as true if the class
takes the parameter too, so it does: `case class Dataset[T](items: List[T])`, with the members
written in terms of `T` — `first(): Option[T]`, `filter(p: T => Boolean): Dataset[T]`,
`map[U](f: T => U): Dataset[U]`. Every one of those annotations is DISCARDED by the front (invariant
I-2 — Tier 0 carries no enforced notion of types), so this changes no behaviour and was measured to
confirm it rather than assumed. It is written correctly now so that the type checker owed by SSC3-15
inherits a prelude that already says what it means; the alternative — leaving `Any` everywhere and
fixing it later — is how a signature ends up describing something nobody re-derived.

```scalascript
case class RuntimeException(message: String):
  def getMessage: String = message

case class Dataset[T](items: List[T]):
  def collect(): List[T] = items
  def count(): Int = items.length
  def isEmpty(): Boolean = items.isEmpty
  def first(): Option[T] = if items.isEmpty then None else Some(items.head)
  def take(n: Int): Dataset[T] = Dataset(items.take(n))
  def map[U](f: T => U): Dataset[U] = Dataset(items.map(x => f(x)))
  def filter(p: T => Boolean): Dataset[T] = Dataset(items.filter(x => p(x)))
  def mkString(sep: String): String = items.mkString(sep)
  def reduce(f: (T, T) => T): T = items.reduce((a, b) => f(a, b))
  def sum(): Any = items.sum
  def avg(): Any = items.sum.toDouble / items.length.toDouble
  def min(): T =
    if items.isEmpty then throw RuntimeException("Dataset.min: empty dataset") else items.min
  def max(): T =
    if items.isEmpty then throw RuntimeException("Dataset.max: empty dataset") else items.max
  def sorted(): Dataset[T] = Dataset(items.sorted)
  def sortBy(f: T => Any): Dataset[T] = Dataset(items.sortBy(x => f(x)))
  def distinct(): Dataset[T] = Dataset(items.distinct)
  def takeOrdered(n: Int): Dataset[T] = Dataset(items.sorted.take(n))
  def top(n: Int): Dataset[T] = Dataset(items.sorted.reverse.take(n))
  def zip[U](other: Dataset[U]): Dataset[Any] = Dataset(items.zip(other.items))
  def flatMap[U](f: T => List[U]): Dataset[U] = Dataset(items.flatMap(x => f(x)))
  def foreach(f: T => Any): Unit = items.foreach(x => f(x))
  // IT RETURNS `(key, reduced)` PAIRS, keeping the key beside the value, and that is not a design
  // choice — the corpus reads it. `dataset-groupBy.ssc` destructures `case (k, v)` and then prints
  // `v._2`, so `v` is itself the reduced ELEMENT, a `(word, count)` pair. Returning just the reduced
  // element — my first version — makes `v` the bare count and `v._2` fails with `method '_2' on 3`.
  //
  // That error cost the longest detour of this file: it survived two wrong hypotheses (placeholder
  // expansion, then eta-reduced `map(key)`) because BOTH spellings work in isolation and so does
  // the whole call reproduced by hand. Only reading the test's LAST TWO LINES explained it. The
  // shape of a returned value is part of the contract, and a probe that stops at "it runs" cannot
  // see it.
  def reduceByKey(key: T => Any)(f: (T, T) => T): Dataset[Any] =
    Dataset(items.map(x => key(x)).distinct
                 .map(k => (k, items.filter(x => key(x) == k).reduce((p, q) => f(p, q)))))
  def zipWithIndex: Dataset[Any] = Dataset(items.zipWithIndex)
  def drop(n: Int): Dataset[T] = Dataset(items.drop(n))
  def fold(z: Any)(f: (Any, T) => Any): Any = items.foldLeft(z)((a, b) => f(a, b))
  def union(other: Dataset[T]): Dataset[T] = Dataset(items ++ other.items)
  def intersect(other: Dataset[T]): Dataset[T] =
    Dataset(items.filter(x => other.items.contains(x)).distinct)
  def groupBy(f: T => Any): Dataset[Any] =
    Dataset(items.map(x => f(x)).distinct.map(k => (k, items.filter(x => f(x) == k))))
  // A CLOSURE, not a Map, and the corpus decides that rather than taste: it only ever APPLIES the
  // result — `freq(k)` — and Tier 0 can neither build a Map from a list (`toMap` is absent) nor add
  // to one (`Map + (k -> v)` is refused). A user-defined `apply` is not callable either. A function
  // value satisfies every use the corpus makes and needs nothing the language does not have.
  def countByValue(): Any => Any = (k: T) => items.filter(x => x == k).length
  def runLocal(): Dataset[T] = Dataset(items)
  def runParallel(): Dataset[T] = Dataset(items)

object Dataset:
  def fromList[T](xs: List[T]): Dataset[T] = Dataset(xs)
  def of[T](items: T*): Dataset[T] = Dataset(items)
```

## The `List` companion

`List.fill`, `List.tabulate` and `List.range` — the members Scala puts on `List`'s companion
object. Measured before writing: `List` is 4 of the corpus's `unknown name` refusals, asked for as
`List.fill(4)(0)`, `List.tabulate(5)(i => i*i)`, `List.range(1, 6)` and `List.range(0, 10, 3)`.

HERE AND NOT IN THE KERNEL, because the project's rule is that new intrinsics go to a library and
never to the core — and a library written in ScalaScript reaches BOTH lanes with no host surface at
all, which is the whole reason the prelude exists.

`object List` COEXISTS with the `List(…)` literal, which `Lower` special-cases: one is the name
`List` and the other is `List.fill`, and they are different entries in the signature table. Probed
before this was written rather than hoped for.

`range` takes its step as a DEFAULTED parameter rather than being declared twice. Two `def`s of one
name would be arity overloading, and `checkArity` resolves a call with `sigs.find` — the FIRST match
by name — so the three-argument call would be checked against the two-argument signature and
refused. One signature with `step: Int = 1` answers both spellings.

Each loop counts DOWN and conses onto the front, so the result comes out in order without a
reverse — `::` is the only list constructor Tier 0 has.

`range` COUNTS FIRST and then builds, which reads as a detour and is the fix for an EMPTY range.
The first version walked up to remember the last value and then walked down from it, so
`List.range(5, 5)` and `List.range(5, 3)` each produced ONE element instead of none: the downward
loop runs at least once whatever the bound. Counting makes emptiness `n = 0`, and a loop that never
starts. The corpus case does not contain an empty range, so this would have shipped behind a green
measurement — the edge probe is what caught it.

```scalascript
object List:
  def fill(n: Int)(x: Any): List[Any] =
    var acc = Nil
    var i = 0
    while i < n do
      acc = x :: acc
      i = i + 1
    acc

  def tabulate(n: Int)(f: Int => Any): List[Any] =
    var acc = Nil
    var i = n - 1
    while i >= 0 do
      acc = f(i) :: acc
      i = i - 1
    acc

  def range(lo: Int, hi: Int, step: Int = 1): List[Int] =
    var acc = Nil
    var n = 0
    var i = lo
    while i < hi do
      n = n + 1
      i = i + step
    i = lo + (n - 1) * step
    while n > 0 do
      acc = i :: acc
      i = i - step
      n = n - 1
    acc
```

## `math`

`math.sqrt`, `math.round`, `math.abs`, `math.Pi`, `math.pow` — 4 of the corpus's `unknown name`
refusals ask for them: `arithmetic`, `case-classes`, `js-scala-fenced-block`, `sealed-traits`.
Measured before writing, and measured again the day it was written, because a verdict about what
is missing is dated evidence.

**The split is decided by what v2's VM already has, not by what is convenient**, exactly as the
host IO table was. `f.sqrt`, `f.floor`, `f.ceil` and `f.round` are prims v2 implements, so they are
reached through an `extern` and both lanes get v2's own answer. `f.pow` does NOT exist in v2, so a
prim for it would run on the executor and be refused by the bridge — the divergence invariant I-3
exists to forbid — and `pow` is written in ScalaScript here instead, where one implementation
serves both lanes.

**`pow` was PARTIAL for a day and is not any more.** It raised on a fractional exponent, because
v2 had no `f.pow` and no `f.exp`/`f.log` either, and every v3-only workaround was ruled out by
measurement: the reference lane is libm-exact (`pow(2.0, 0.1)` = 1.0717734625362931), so a
ScalaScript series or the repeated-sqrt expansion — tempting, since both lanes already had
`f.sqrt` — differs in the last bits. The owner's decision of 2026-08-14 put `f.pow` in v2's kernel
beside `f.sqrt`, across all five backends and the Core IR spec, so this is now one libm call on
every lane.

**`round` follows v2 to the letter and that is not pedantry.** v2 implements `f.round` as `rint` —
half-to-EVEN, returning a Double — and not as Scala's `math.round`, which is half-up and returns a
Long. The two agree on every value in the corpus and disagree at exactly `.5`, so taking the
familiar name would have left a divergence no fixture here can see.

**The four host hooks are declared at TOP LEVEL, not inside the object, and since 2026-08-15 that
is a CHOICE rather than a limitation.** It used to be a limitation: neither front carried `extern`
into an `object`, which is `v3-extern-member-in-an-object-has-no-meaning`, now fixed — both fronts
carry it and `Lower` resolves it under the qualified name. What keeps the declarations out here is
the DELEGATING BODY, and specifically its `.toDouble`: v2's `flt` refuses an Int, so `math.sqrt(16)`
died on the bridge while the executor answered 4. That widening is one line of `.ssc` that both
lanes run. Writing `object math: extern def sqrt(…)` would move it into a builder in Scala, which is
a worse place for it. The `__` prefix keeps these names out of the way of a program that defines its
own `sqrt` — this file loads for EVERY program.

```scalascript
extern def __mathPow(b: Double, e: Double): Double
extern def __mathSqrt(x: Double): Double
extern def __mathFloor(x: Double): Double
extern def __mathCeil(x: Double): Double
extern def __mathRound(x: Double): Double

object math:
  val Pi: Double = 3.141592653589793
  val E: Double = 2.718281828459045

  // `.toDouble` IS LOAD-BEARING and was added after a probe, not before. v2's `flt` REFUSES an
  // Int — `math.sqrt(16)` died on the bridge with `expected Float, got 16` while the executor
  // answered 4 — so the widening has to happen ABOVE the prim, in one line both lanes run, rather
  // than inside either lane's copy of it.
  def sqrt(x: Double): Double = __mathSqrt(x.toDouble)
  def floor(x: Double): Double = __mathFloor(x.toDouble)
  def ceil(x: Double): Double = __mathCeil(x.toDouble)
  // `round` IS NOT THE `f.round` PRIM, and this line is where the two are kept apart.
  //
  // The prim breaks a tie to EVEN (IEEE-754 `rint`, the Core IR contract in
  // `v2/specs/10-core-ir.md`). Scala's `math.round` breaks it UP and returns an integer, and so do
  // all five other lanes of this repository — measured 2026-08-16 on `math.round(2.5 / 3.5 / -2.5 /
  // -3.5)`: `--v1`, native, `--bytecode`, `run-js` and `run-jvm` every one printed `3 4 -2 -3`,
  // against `2 4 -2 -4` here. Delegating to `__mathRound` imported the PRIM's rule into a LANGUAGE
  // function, which is how one program got two answers.
  //
  // TWO DIFFERENCES, NOT ONE, and the second is easy to miss: the result TYPE. `math.round(5.0) / 2`
  // printed `2` on the other lanes (integer division) and `2.5` here, so the divergence survived
  // anywhere the value was used rather than printed. Hence `Int`, not `Double`.
  //
  // `floor(x + 0.5)` IS the half-up rule, including for negatives — `-2.5` gives `floor(-2.0)` =
  // `-2` and `-3.5` gives `floor(-3.0)` = `-3`, which is what the five lanes print. `.toDouble`
  // before the add for the reason the `sqrt` comment above gives: v2's `flt` refuses an Int.
  //
  // `__mathFloor` AND NOT `floor`, which is the member two lines up: a bare member name does not
  // resolve inside its own object here — `call to unknown function 'floor'` — and the delegating
  // members all reach the top-level hook for the same reason.
  // (v2/BUGS.md `math-round-and-f-round-disagree-at-a-tie`.)
  def round(x: Double): Int = __mathFloor(x.toDouble + 0.5).toInt

  // `abs` is written over `Any` because Tier 0 has no overloading — two `def`s of one name leave
  // the first winning — and the corpus calls it on an Int (`math.abs(-7)` is 7, an Int, not 7.0).
  // Comparing and negating are both defined for either, so one body serves both.
  def abs(x: Any): Any = if x < 0 then -x else x

  def max(a: Any, b: Any): Any = if a > b then a else b
  def min(a: Any, b: Any): Any = if a < b then a else b

  // ONE libm call on every lane, since `f.pow` became a kernel prim (2026-08-14). It used to be a
  // multiply loop that RAISED on a fractional exponent, because v2 had no prim and an
  // approximation would have disagreed with the reference lane in the last bits — the loop and its
  // refusal are gone with the reason for them.
  def pow(b: Double, e: Double): Double = __mathPow(b.toDouble, e.toDouble)

// ── What a coroutine answers ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// DECLARED HERE BECAUSE v3's TYPE TABLE IS CLOSED AND v2's IS NOT. `coroutineResume` is a host
// function from generator-plugin and it answers `DataV("Yielded", …)`; v2 names a constructor with a
// STRING, so a tag the program never declared prints and matches there without ceremony. v3 numbers
// its constructors — a `VData` is an INDEX into this program's type table — so a tag with no entry
// cannot be represented at all, and the adapter refused it at run time with no position: six corpus
// cases went from an honest refusal to a CRASH, which is the worse half of the floor.
//
// Declaring the three constructors is what makes them ordinary: `Yielded(1)` prints, and
// `case Errored(_) =>` matches, through exactly the same machinery as any other case class. No
// executor change and no new value kind — the host's data is v3's data once v3 knows the name.
case class Yielded[A](value: A)
case class Returned[A](value: A)
case class Errored[A](error: A)
```

## Optics

**THE OPTIC VALUES ARE NOT DEFINED HERE.** They live in [`std/optics.ssc`](../../std/optics.ssc) —
ordinary ScalaScript, no host surface, and MEASURED to give byte-identical output on the v2 reference
lane and on both v3 lanes. The prelude imports them so `Focus`/`Prism` need no import at the use
site, which is the only thing this file adds.

**WHY A SHARED FILE AND NOT A PRELUDE SECTION.** The reference implements optics as a host plugin
(`v2/runtime/std/optics-plugin`), because a path step that reads a field BY NAME needs reflection.
The library needs none: a FIELD step is handed a getter and a setter, and whoever knows the field
names supplies them — a front that reads `_.address.city` knows them at compile time. Everything
else (`.some`, `.each`, `.index`, `.at`) is Option, List and Map, which need no names. So the
semantics can be one file every version shares, and only the syntax stays per-version. That is the
choice the owner already took for `Dataset` (`BACKLOG.md`, option 3): write the library IN
ScalaScript so every lane gets one implementation and none grows host surface.

[Optic, opticField, opticSome, opticEach, opticIndex, opticAt, opticShow, opticJoinKind, opticMissing, opticSomeAll, opticSomeOver, opticAtAll, opticAtOver, opticAtLabel, opticIndexUpdate](../../std/optics.ssc)

**`PrismOptic` STAYS HERE, and the asymmetry is honest:** a prism tests a variant TAG, which a
`case c: Circle =>` pattern does and a portable library cannot express without knowing the name. The
rewrite emits that pattern, so what is left for a value to hold is two closures — small enough that a
shared file would carry nothing but a constructor.

```scalascript
case class PrismOptic(test: Any, wrap: Any, _show: String):
  def getOption(s: Any): Any = test(s)
  def modify(s: Any, f: Any): Any =
    test(s) match
      case Some(x) => wrap(f(x))
      case None => s
  def set(s: Any, v: Any): Any =
    test(s) match
      case Some(_) => wrap(v)
      case None => s
  def reverseGet(x: Any): Any = wrap(x)
```

## HTML

**`html"…"` and `raw(v)` live in [`std/html.ssc`](../../std/html.ssc)**, for the same reason the
optics do: `pfx"…"` lowers to `pfx(parts, args)`, so an interpolator IS an ordinary function and
belongs in a file every version can read. The reference does the escaping in the FRONT — every hole
wrapped in the `__htmlEscape__` prim — which is a front's decision the language no longer has to
take.

[html, raw, htmlEscape, htmlValue, HtmlRaw](../../std/html.ssc)

## md

**`md"…"` is an ordinary function, and that is the whole point of the interpolator decision** —
`v3-an-interpolator-prefix-is-hardcoded-in-both-fronts` (fixed f92e3c644) made every prefix except
`s` lower to `pfx(parts, args)`, so an interpolator is defined by defining a function. `md` is the
first one this repository actually defines, and it needs no front change, no prim and no plugin.

**THE STRIP RULE IS THE REFERENCE'S, VERBATIM** — `v2/src/Runtime.scala`'s `__mdStrip__`, itself
copied from v1's `Interpreter.stripIndent`: drop blank lines at both edges, then remove the MINIMUM
leading-space indent of the non-blank body lines, and blank interior lines become empty rather than
keeping their spaces. Copied rather than re-derived because `tests/conformance/expected/content.txt`
is the reference's own output, and a re-derivation is how a library comes to disagree with the
goldens it is measured against.

**Written over a `List[String]`, not with `dropRight`**, which this lane does not implement — the
same rule with `take(n - 1)` says the same thing and runs on both.

```scalascript
def mdStrip(s: String): String =
  val lines = s.split("\n")
  var body = lines
  while body.length > 0 && body(0).trim == "" do body = body.drop(1)
  while body.length > 0 && body(body.length - 1).trim == "" do body = body.take(body.length - 1)
  if body.length == 0 then ""
  else
    var minIndent = 1000000
    body.foreach { l =>
      if l.trim != "" then
        var i = 0
        while i < l.length && l.charAt(i) == ' ' do i = i + 1
        if i < minIndent then minIndent = i
    }
    body.map(l => if l.trim == "" then "" else l.substring(minIndent)).mkString("\n")

def md(parts: List[String], args: List[Any]): String =
  var out = ""
  var i = 0
  parts.foreach { p =>
    out = out + p
    if i < args.length then out = out + args(i).toString
    i = i + 1
  }
  mdStrip(out)
```
